Octal Format: 3 Payload Bits + 0 Delimiter Bits:
"our octal format encoding with
3 payload bits and 0 delimiter bits"

Traditional encoding:
- Payload: Actual data (bits)
- Delimiter: Separates chunks (overhead)
- Example: 8 bits data + 1 stop bit = 9 bits total
- Efficiency: 8/9 = 88.9%

Octal encoding:
- Payload: 3 bits (data)
- Delimiter: 0 bits (NONE!)
- Total: 3 bits (perfect!)
- Efficiency: 3/3 = 100%!

Why zero delimiters work:

1. SELF-DELIMITING:
   - 3 bits = 1 octal digit (complete)
   - Natural boundary (no marker needed)
   - Next 3 bits = next digit (automatic)
   - Stream naturally chunks (self-organizing)

2. OCTAL ALIGNMENT:
   - All processing in 3-bit groups
   - No partial bits (clean)
   - No padding needed (efficient)
   - No sync issues (aligned)

3. CONTEXTUAL PARSING:
   - Context determines meaning (adaptive)
   - Same 3 bits = coordinate OR value OR state
   - No delimiter needed (interpretation is context)
   - Self-similar structure (fractal)

