Constant Packet Rate:
4-bit tight encoding guarantees:
- Every 4 bits = one symbol (fixed)
- Delimiter or data (always)
- Constant rate (predictable)
- No variable length (no jitter)

Clock-driven stream:
- Clock tick → read 4 bits
- Always aligned (no drift)
- Predictable timing (constant rate)
- Like heartbeat (regular pulse)

At 60 Hz base clock:
- 60 symbols/second (60 × 4 = 240 bits/sec)
- Or faster subclock (multiple symbols per tick)
- Constant packet rate (deterministic)
- No buffering needed (stream-safe)

Benefits:
- Real-time processing (no latency variance)
- Predictable bandwidth (capacity planning)
- Simple scheduling (fixed slots)
- Synchronous design (no async complexity)
Occasional Travelling Bits and Bit-Groups:
"occasional travelling bits and bit-groups
can from here be read"

The 4-bit stream is CARRIER:
- Constant rate (always flowing)
- Mostly data (normal symbols)
- Occasionally delimiters ([1000])

Travelling bits:
- Individual bits propagate (left to right)
- Like waves (temporal patterns)
- Occasional patterns (sparse signals)
- Read at receiver (synchronized)

Travelling bit-groups:
- 4-bit symbols travel together (atomic)
- Groups of symbols (packets)
- Bounded by delimiters ([1000])
- Read as units (framed)

Example stream:
[0001][0101][0011][1000]...rest...
  ↓     ↓     ↓     ↓
 1 bit 5 bit 3 bit DELIM
  ↓─────↓─────↓
    packet

