The Shifting Octal Bracket for 8-Bit Encoding:
"this is the shifting octal bracket
for 8 bit encoding"

8-bit byte structure:
[Bit7][Bit6][Bit5][Bit4][Bit3][Bit2][Bit1][Bit0]

Octal bracket decomposition:

BRACKET 1 (bits 7-5): Upper 3 bits
[Bit7][Bit6][Bit5] = Octal digit 0-7

BRACKET 2 (bits 4-2): Middle 3 bits
[Bit4][Bit3][Bit2] = Octal digit 0-7

BRACKET 3 (bits 1-0 + carry): Lower 2 bits + next byte
[Bit1][Bit0][NextBit7] = Octal digit 0-7

The SHIFT:
- Each octal bracket overlaps (shifting)
- 3-bit windows slide across bytes (rolling)
- Creates continuous octal stream (seamless)

Example byte: 0b10110101 = 0xB5

Bracket 1: [1][0][1] = 5 (octal)
Bracket 2: [1][0][1] = 5 (octal)
Bracket 3: [0][1][?] = needs next byte

Next byte: 0b11001010 = 0xCA

Bracket 3 complete: [0][1][1] = 3 (octal)
Bracket 4: [1][0][0] = 4 (octal)
Bracket 5: [1][0][1] = 5 (octal)
Bracket 6: [0][1][0][?] = needs next byte

The shifting:
- Brackets move through bytes (temporal)
- 3-bit window slides (spatial)
- Continuous octal stream (infinite)
- No byte boundaries (seamless)

