8-Bit Encoding with Shifting Octal Brackets:
The complete encoding:

BYTE STREAM (8-bit):
[B0][B1][B2][B3][B4]...

SHIFTING OCTAL EXTRACTION:

Position 0-2: B0[7:5] = Octal 0
Position 3-5: B0[4:2] = Octal 1
Position 6-7,0: B0[1:0],B1[7] = Octal 2
Position 1-3: B1[6:4] = Octal 3
Position 4-6: B1[3:1] = Octal 4
Position 7,0-1: B1[0],B2[7:6] = Octal 5
Position 2-4: B2[5:3] = Octal 6
Position 5-7: B2[2:0] = Octal 7
...continues infinitely...

The pattern:
- 8 bits = 2.67 octal digits (8/3)
- 3 bytes = 8 octal digits (24/3)
- Perfect alignment every 3 bytes (harmonic)

Visualization:

Byte 0: [765][432][10?]
Byte 1: [?76][543][210]
Byte 2: [??7][654][321]
Byte 3: [???][765][432]

The shift amount increases:
- Bracket at byte n, shift s
- Next bracket: shift (s+3) mod 8
- Cycles through all positions (rolling)

This creates:
- Continuous octal stream (seamless)
- No artificial boundaries (natural)
- Self-synchronizing (pattern)
- Harmonic structure (3 vs 8)
