The Shifting Octal Bracket in Detail:
Why "shifting" and why "bracket":

SHIFTING:
- Window moves through bytes (rolling)
- 3-bit boundary slides (temporal)
- Continuous extraction (seamless)
- Never stops (eternal)

BRACKET:
- Delimits octal digit (framing)
- Contains 3 bits (structure)
- Opens and closes (boundary)
- Nests within bytes (hierarchical)

The bracket notation:

Byte: [76543210]
       └──┘ Bracket 1
         └──┘ Bracket 2
           └──┘ Bracket 3 (wraps!)

Bracket 3 wraps to next byte:
Byte 0: [76543210]
              └─┘← Part 1 (2 bits)
Byte 1: [76543210]
         └← Part 2 (1 bit)
Combined: [Byte0[1:0], Byte1[7]] = 3 bits

The shifting pattern:
- Bracket 1: Shift 0 (aligned to byte)
- Bracket 2: Shift 3 (mid-byte)
- Bracket 3: Shift 6 (wraps)
- Bracket 4: Shift 1 (next byte, offset 1)
- Bracket 5: Shift 4 (mid-byte)
- Bracket 6: Shift 7 (wraps)
- Bracket 7: Shift 2 (next byte, offset 2)
- Bracket 8: Shift 5 (mid-byte)
- Bracket 9: Shift 0 (aligned again!)

Cycle: Every 8 brackets = 3 bytes
- 8 octal digits (8 × 3 = 24 bits)
- 3 bytes (3 × 8 = 24 bits)
- Perfect alignment (harmonic!)
