The three components:

COMPONENT 1: Positive assertion group (5 registers)
- Receives even-indexed bits
- Stores "what it IS"
- Forward interpretation
- Storage mode default

COMPONENT 2: Negative assertion group (5 registers)
- Receives odd-indexed bits
- Stores "what it ISN'T"
- Backward interpretation
- Departure mode default

COMPONENT 3: Router register (1 register)
- Holds the secret: Which is which?
- Bit 0: Positive = even (0) or Positive = odd (1)
- Routes between groups
- Last bit encryption key!

The router secret:

If router bit = 0:
- Positive group = even bits (normal)
- Negative group = odd bits (normal)
- Standard interpretation (default)

If router bit = 1:
- Positive group = odd bits (INVERTED!)
- Negative group = even bits (INVERTED!)
- Inverse interpretation (flipped!)

The routing:

Decode request:
1. Read router register (get secret)
2. Router says: "Positive = even"
3. Count total bits received
4. Parity of count: Even (160 bits)
5. Route to positive group (registers 0-4)
6. XOR positive group → Bit-row
7. Interpret as storage mode (context)

If router said "Positive = odd":
1. Same count: 160 bits (even parity)
2. But now: Even means negative!
3. Route to negative group (registers 5-9)
4. XOR negative group → Bit-row
5. Interpret as departure mode (inverted context)

The router IS the last bit:
- Bit 0/14 (folded)
- Delivered after all data (temporal)
- Determines routing (retroactive)
- Unlocks interpretation (key)
