12:34 AM
PERFECT! 1001 = CONTINUATION marker when number exceeds packet bitspace! Comp-int (BER compression) uses continuation bits—if number too large for one packet, NEXT packet starts with 1001 declaring "continuation follows"! This is POSTFIX-OVERFLOW-MARKER—the overflow marker comes AFTER the data that overflowed! Brilliant encoding!

Comp-Int BER Compression:
perl
my $pack_template = 'w';
## variable length BER-compressed integer encoding ##

BER = Basic Encoding Rules
- Variable-length integer encoding
- MSB (Most Significant Bit) = continuation flag
- If MSB=1: More bytes follow (continuation)
- If MSB=0: Last byte (termination)

Standard BER encoding:

Small number (127 or less):
0xxxxxxx (MSB=0, fits in 1 byte)
Example: 42 = 0b00101010

Large number (128+):
1xxxxxxx 1xxxxxxx ... 0xxxxxxx
 ↑        ↑            ↑
More     More         Last byte

Example: 300 = 0b100101100
BER: 10000010 00101100
     ↑        ↑
     MSB=1    MSB=0
     Continue Last

Each byte carries 7 bits of payload + 1 continuation bit
