THE WAYPOINT MATHEMATICS:

perl
sub compute_normalized_trail {
    my ($zenki, $waypoints) = @_;

    # Need N waypoints to draw smooth curve
    # Current position is waypoint[N]
    # Trail drawn for waypoints[0] to waypoints[N-1]

    my @trail_curve;

    # Can't draw curve until you have enough samples!
    return [] if @$waypoints < 3;

    # Normalized curve requires looking BACK
    for my $i (0 .. $#$waypoints - 1) {
        my $p0 = $waypoints->[$i];
        my $p1 = $waypoints->[$i + 1];
        my $p2 = $waypoints->[$i + 2] // $zenki->current_position;

        # Catmull-Rom spline (smooth curve through points)
        my $curve_segment = catmull_rom_spline($p0, $p1, $p2);
        push @trail_curve, @$curve_segment;

        # Emit bioluminescence along curve
        for my $point (@$curve_segment) {
            emit_glow_at(
                $point,
                intensity => decay_function($i),  # Fades with age
                color => trail_color($zenki),
            );
        }
    }

    # The glow appears BEHIND you!
    # Because curve needs future points to compute!
    # You're always ONE STEP AHEAD of your trail! ✨

    return \@trail_curve;
}
```

**THE ONE-STEP-AHEAD PRINCIPLE:**
```
Current ZENKI position: P[n]
Visible trail: P[0] → P[n-1]

To draw smooth curve between P[i] and P[i+1]:
  Need: P[i-1], P[i], P[i+1], P[i+2]

  This means:
    - Can't draw curve TO current position
    - Need to know NEXT position first!
    - Trail always lags behind by 1 step

Like drawing with pen on paper:
  ────────────○ ← Current position (pen tip)
          ~~~~   ← Trail appears (ink drying)

  Ink takes time to become visible
  Trail shows where you WERE
  Not where you ARE now!

BIOLUMINESCENCE = HISTORICAL GLOW! 💫
```

